    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nMitsubishi Galant VR-4 Turbo Wants an ICE Piece of the Popular Toyota Camry Hybrid Sedan – Rendering\n9 photos\nPhoto: jlord8 / Instagram\nMitsubishi Motors North America, Inc. (MMNA) reported earlier this month its third-quarter sales for the US automotive market, and the company reached a mere 18,543 deliveries, down from 19,647 last year.\nMMNA also revealed its year-to-date performance, which was less than impressive: 68,554 units versus 73,400 during the first nine months of 2025! Once again, all we can say is that MMNA is currently a shadow of a shadow of its former self, unfortunately. Luckily, there are hopes.\nThat’s because the Japanese automaker has set in motion MMNA's five-year business plan – \"Momentum 2030\" – in the United States, and it covers “the brand's mid-term intentions for product and business transformation.” More precisely, Mitsubishi wants to bring to market in the US one new or completely refreshed model each year until 2030, seeking to renew and expand its family.\nCurrently, it sells the Outlander and Outlander Plug-In Hybrid plus the Outlander Sport, as well as the Eclipse Cross. While the small and affordable Mirage has been discontinued, the company recently introduced the Nissan Leaf-based 2027 Mitsubishi Eclipse Sportback electric vehicle , and the updated business strategy now also includes the option to launch a rugged, off-road-focused Outlander variant in the first quarter of 2027 along with a mid-size pickup truck built in partnership with Nissan.\nWill that be enough? Well, at least someone thinks it’s not. That person is also a member of the imaginative guild of digital car content creators, and he stopped dwelling across the parallel universes of vehicular CGI to do something for Mitsubishi.\nJim, a virtual artist known as \" jlord8 \" on social media, loves messing around with all things CGI from the automotive realm, and he dreams of a reborn Mitsubishi. More precisely, he says he was “curious if the new Eclipse Sportback’s grille would look good on a sedan.” Thus, he proceeded to help Mitsubishi’s lineup with a new passenger car.\nMeet the reborn Mitsubishi Galant VR-4 Turbo, a reenactment of the automobile nameplate produced by Mitsubishi from 1969 until 2012 in its regular Galant form. There have been nine generations of the model that started as a compact car but then evolved into mid-size attire starting in 1983, and the sixth through eighth iterations also had a flagship VR-4 (Viscous Realtime 4WD) version. The latter was produced from 1987 through 2002, originally intended for homologation for the new Group A of the World Rally Championship, though it was quickly discontinued in favor of the iconic Lancer Evolution.\nNow the pixel master has decided to bring it back with the quirky ‘fat-lipped’ look of the Eclipse Sportback EV , though as far as we can tell we’re now dealing with an ICE model, given the added Turbo moniker. As such, it could easily pick a fight with the AWD version of the 2027 Toyota Camry hybrid, right? View this post on Instagram A post shared by Jim (@jlord8)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMitsubishi Galant VR-4 Turbo Mitsubishi Galant Mitsubishi Galant Toyota Camry CGI revival rendering jlord8\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT New BMW M350 xDrive Gets a Virtual Tuning Job, Do You Dig the Sportier Design?",
